firebase is on the decline and by that i  mean it's javascript bundle size one of  the biggest criticisms developers have  about firebase is that it requires a lot  of kilobytes of javascript to get the  job done  a large bundle size can lead to poor  performance and a bad user experience  but i have some good news  firebase is releasing a brand new  javascript sdk that can dramatically  reduce the size of your web application  in today's video you'll learn how to  upgrade your existing firebase app to  reduce the bundle size by more than 50  and i'll also give you my first  impressions about what i like and  dislike about this new sdk  because it's filled with breaking  changes but first if you're serious  about building a firebase app  consider upgrading to pro on fireship io  to get access to my full project-based  courses  as developers we always want to use the  latest and greatest technology  gentlemen we can rebuild him we have the  technology  but at the same time we hate dealing  with breaking changes the new firebase  sdk makes it possible to build apps with  a much smaller javascript footprint but  it comes at the cost of a lot of  breaking changes that you'll have to  refactor in your code the problem is  that firebase has been around for a long  time  and it uses what are known as side  effect imports you import and initialize  your firebase app which creates a global  firebase object then extend it with  additional  functionality by importing other modules  like auth firestore storage and so on  this creates a lot of bloat  because these imports add a bunch of  functionality that you're probably not  going to use in your application for  example  your app might use email password login  but importing the auth module  adds the code for every other sign in  method on the platform which is nothing  but dead code that your end user has to  download from the network  instead of accessing methods on one  giant object with dot notation the new  sdk takes a functional approach  allowing you to import the exact  functionality that you need  and that means everything else can be  excluded from the javascript bundle in  other words it's a tree shakeable  library now another cool thing in the  new sdk  is that there's a firestore light  package firestore is the biggest  module in firebase but if you want to  use the database and don't need things  like real-time listeners or offline mode  you can use the light package which is  84 smaller than the normal package  that's pretty awesome but now let's jump  into the code and see what these  breaking changes  actually look like i'm using vt as my  build tool and the first thing i'll do  is install the firebase sdk using the  beta tag that'll install version 9 of  the sdk  but the beta tag may not be required  depending on when you're watching this  video the only important thing is that  version  9 is installed now if i go into the  javascript file it's currently empty  giving us a bundle size of 0.  the first thing i'll do is import  firebase the old way the nice thing  about this sdk  is that it gives you a compat library  allowing you to upgrade your code base  with minimal modifications to your  existing code  as you can see here i'm performing a  side effect import for  auth firestore and storage this should  look familiar  minus the compat part if you've ever  used firebase before if we go ahead and  build the app you can see that our  bundle size  is now 630 kilobytes which triggers a  warning  and broadly that compressor won't even  touch it that's kind of demoralizing if  you care about performance let's go  ahead and remove this code and look at  how tree shakeable imports work in the  new sdk  instead of a side effect import we  import the functionality that we  actually need from each individual  module  like if we want to sign up a user with  google we can bring in sign in with  pop-up  and sign out from the auth module or  upload bytes from storage to upload a  file  now one thing that's kind of weird is  that you'll notice we import a function  called get auth get storage and get  firestore for each one of these modules  then after initializing the app we need  to call that function  to work with the actual module itself  this is like an initialization step  that is required because it's possible  to have multiple firebase projects used  in a single front-end application  if you are using multiple firebase  projects you can pass the firebase app  as an argument  to give that module the proper context  in the old sdk  you would have used dot notation on the  firebase app to access the feature that  you're working with now if we go ahead  and build the app at this point you can  see our bundle size has been reduced all  the way down to 206 kilobytes  that's a third of the size it was  previously or 42 kilobytes compressed  which is a much more reasonable starting  point considering we have all kinds of  functionality ready to go in this app  now let's go ahead and put one of these  functions to use like sign in with  pop-up now in order to use it  we pass the auth object as the first  argument then the provider which might  be something like facebook or github  as the second argument one thing i don't  really love here is that for every  function  we need to pass auth as the first  argument it's not really any more code  than dot notation  but it just feels kind of awkward in my  opinion i find myself wanting to create  my own custom abstraction  like a class or maybe just a helper  function to get rid of that auth object  altogether  maybe that's just me but i'm curious  what you guys think about the ergonomics  of this stk so let me know  in the comments if you have an existing  project your breaking changes can be  overcome by simply refactoring your dot  notation to this more functional  approach  that's not very difficult but it will  require a lot of tedious code changes  overall i think this new sdk is a great  direction for firebase  and will allow them to add new  functionality to the sdk without  increasing your bundle size every time  and most importantly  it puts an end to the criticism that  firebase is just too big  i'm going to go ahead and wrap things up  there become a pro member to see  firebase in action  thanks for watching and i will see you  in the next one
 

